{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779335685,"nonce":"c+uhHLHKsk+wmQ2AYKU7d3ki6Qw=","title":"Studio: resolve UI wrapper vs runtime component representation boundary","message":"## Problem\n\nStudio currently straddles two graph representations. Some saved/runtime payloads use backend component types such as `SimpleStagedNetwork`, while the UI expects authoring wrappers such as `Network` that can expose an editable subgraph affordance. This boundary is currently handled by migration in places, but the intended canonical representation is not explicit enough.\n\nA recent bug showed the risk: restored project tabs skipped graph migration, so the RLRMP movement-ramp project rendered `SimpleStagedNetwork` directly and lost the Network subgraph button. The immediate fix was to run `migrateGraphSpec()` during snapshot restore, but the larger representation rule should be made deliberate.\n\n## Context\n\nKnown current behavior:\n\n- backend/worker/runtime code may accept or emit `SimpleStagedNetwork`;\n- Studio graph authoring treats `Network` as the composite wrapper with subgraph internals;\n- `migrateGraphSpec()` maps `SimpleStagedNetwork` to `Network` and normalizes related ports/params;\n- saved projects, examples, local tabs, imports, and worker-generated payloads may enter through different hydration/restore paths;\n- if any path skips migration, UI affordances and validation semantics can diverge from the intended authoring model.\n\nThis issue is not only about Network. The same boundary can recur for CDE templates, Penzai adapters, acausal systems, composite nodes, and future provider-owned components.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Decide the canonical Studio authoring representation versus runtime/lowered representation for composite components.\n- Document where migration belongs: backend serialization, API boundary, frontend hydrate/restore, local tab persistence, import/export, or all of the above.\n- Ensure every graph ingress path applies the same normalization/migration rules.\n- Define how UI wrapper nodes map to runtime components during worker/spec lowering.\n- Prevent UI-only wrapper details from leaking into runtime where inappropriate, and prevent runtime-only component names from degrading authoring affordances.\n- Add tests around saved examples, restored local tabs, imported graphs, and backend payloads containing runtime component names.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- `Network` vs `SimpleStagedNetwork` has an explicit contract: what is saved, what is shown in Studio, and what is lowered for execution.\n- All graph restore/hydration paths normalize to the same authoring representation before rendering.\n- Composite affordances such as `Open subgraph` are stable after save/load, local tab restore, example import, and backend reload.\n- Export/runtime lowering can still produce or consume backend component types intentionally.\n- Regression tests cover at least the RLRMP movement-ramp graph and one generic persisted `SimpleStagedNetwork` graph.\n\n## Related Issues\n\n- `4178e12` — worker graph/spec lowering and graph-as-model violations.\n- `c77b227` — composite cleanup umbrella, including Network subgraph behavior.\n- `2f8dd61` — parametric composite nodes where outer params drive inner graph generation.\n- `6116155` — typed subgraph taxonomy and pluggable canvas architecture.\n- `c6c6da0` — Feedbax manifests/provider contract.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}